ith the Soviet Union’s inaugural appearance) to capture three medals in total."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Finland, the host nation, won its first Olympic rowing medal by taking bronze in the coxless fours, as did Yugoslavia and Czechoslovakia by taking gold in the coxless and coxed fours respectively. Two new entities, Saar and the Soviet Union, appeared in rowing events in Helsinki, although both had appeared in other forms at previous editions. Chile and Romania made their débuts by entering competitors in the single sculls and the coxed eights respectively. These nations raised the number of participating countries to a record of 33 and helped increase the distribution of medals to a greater degree than in any previous Olympics."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Events"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:thead {} "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Event"] "\n" [:th {} "Status"] "\n" [:th {} "Date"] "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:th {} "NOCs"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/158111"} "Single Sculls, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "21 – 23 July 1952"] "\n" [:td {} "18"] "\n" [:td {} "18"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/158130"} "Double Sculls, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "20 – 23 July 1952"] "\n" [:td {} "32"] "\n" [:td {} "16"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/158147"} "Coxless Pairs, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "20 – 23 July 1952"] "\n" [:td {} "32"] "\n" [:td {} "16"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/158164"} "Coxed Pairs, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "20 – 23 July 1952"] "\n" [:td {} "45"] "\n" [:td {} "15"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/158181"} "Coxless Fours, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "20 – 23 July 1952"] "\n" [:td {} "68"] "\n" [:td {} "17"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/results/158200"} "Coxed Fours, Men"]] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic"] "\n" [:td {} "20 